    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p55-cc-1-6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "The domain of $\\tan x$ is all real $x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. $\\tan x$ is undefined at odd multiples of $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.",
          "worked": "False. The tangent curve has vertical asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+k\\pi$, where $k\\in\\mathbb Z$. These values are excluded from its domain.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p55-cc-1-6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "The period of $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$ is $2\\pi$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Each sine or cosine cycle repeats after a horizontal translation of $2\\pi$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p55-cc-1-6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "The period of $\\tan x$ is $\\pi$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. The tangent curve repeats after a horizontal translation of $\\pi$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p55-cc-1-6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "The amplitude of $\\tan x$ is $\\infty$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. The tangent curve has no defined amplitude.",
          "worked": "False. Amplitude is measured from the mean value to a maximum or minimum. The tangent curve is unbounded and has no maximum or minimum, so its amplitude is undefined.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p55-cc-1-6-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "The amplitude of a $\\sin x$ or $\\cos x$ curve is the distance between the maximum and minimum values.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. The amplitude is half that distance.",
          "worked": "False. Amplitude is the distance from the mean value to a maximum or minimum. For $\\sin x$ and $\\cos x$, the maximum is $1$ and the minimum is $-1$.\n\n$$\\text{Amplitude}=\\frac{1-(-1)}{2}=1.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p55-cc-1-6-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "The mean value is the average of the maximum and minimum values.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. For a sine or cosine curve, the mean value is halfway between the maximum and minimum values.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
